data.content:
Here are the **three EF Core 10 breaking changes** that affect your code:

---

## 1. `ExecuteUpdateAsync` — now accepts a regular lambda

The expression tree overload is gone. Your complex `Expression.Lambda`/`Expression.Call` construction no longer compiles. Replace with a simple lambda:

```csharp
// .NET 10 fix
await context.Blogs.ExecuteUpdateAsync(s =>
{
    s.SetProperty(b => b.Views, 8);
    if (nameChanged)
    {
        s.SetProperty(b => b.Name, "foo");
    }
});
```

No manual expression tree building needed — conditionals work directly inside the lambda body now.

---

## 2. Complex type column name uniquification

EF Core 10 changes column naming in two ways:

- **Duplicate property names** across complex types (e.g., `ShippingAddress.Street` and `BillingAddress.Street`) get a numeric suffix appended.
- **Nested complex types** use the full path (`Complex_NestedComplex_Property` instead of `NestedComplex_Property`), so `Order.Payment.BillingAddress.City` maps to `Payment_BillingAddress_City`.

Running `dotnet ef migrations add` will generate column renames. **Fix:** pin old names explicitly:

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>(b => {
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress, sa =>
        sa.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("ShippingAddress_Street"));
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.BillingAddress, ba =>
        ba.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("BillingAddress_Street"));
});

// For nested: preserve old column name
modelBuilder.Entity<Order>(b => {
    b.ComplexProperty(o => o.Payment, p =>
        p.ComplexProperty(pay => pay.BillingAddress, ba =>
            ba.Property(a => a.City).HasColumnName("BillingAddress_City")));
});
```

---

## 3. `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` — new `logCommandText` parameter

All methods gained a `string logCommandText` parameter (redacted SQL for safe logging). Add it to your implementation:

```csharp
public class MyLogger : IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger
{
    public InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> CommandReaderExecuting(
        IRelationalConnection connection, DbCommand command,
        DbContext context, Guid commandId, Guid connectionId,
        DateTimeOffset startTime,
        string logCommandText)   // ← new parameter
    {
        // Use logCommandText for logging (constants may be redacted)
        // Use command.CommandText for actual SQL
    }
}
```

---

**Summary:** The `ExecuteUpdateAsync` change is the biggest win — it eliminates all that expression tree plumbing. The other two are mechanical: add `HasColumnName()` to prevent schema renames, and add the new parameter to your diagnostics logger.
